{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.IdealTetObtuseReduction","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Archimedean.IdealTetObtuseReduction","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Archimedean.LobachevskyMaximum"],"declarations":3,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.lob_sum_eq_reflected","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.lob_sum_eq_reflected","kind":"theorem","line":109,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/IdealTetObtuseReduction.lean#L109-L137","doc":"**The Λ-sum in reflected form.**  From `γ = π − (α+β)` and\n`Lob_pi_sub : Λ(π − t) = −Λ(t)`,\n\n    Λ(α) + Λ(β) + Λ(γ)  =  Λ(α) + Λ(β) − Λ(α+β).\n\nNo acuteness needed for the identity; combined with `third_angle_acute_of_obtuse`\nthe right-hand side has all three arguments (`α`, `β`, `α+β`) acute exactly when\n`γ` is the obtuse one.  This is Milnor's \"one piece enters with a minus sign\" at\nthe analytic level.\n\n**The geometric subdivision that realises it is no longer open** (this docstring\nsaid it was, until `qou-w3eg` H4-5 landed).  It is\n`ObtuseTiling.obtuseWedges_ae_eq_inscribedTriangle` — the triangle is a.e. the\ntwo surviving wedges minus the reflected one — and this lemma is what that\nfile's `hypVolume_chimney_inscribedTriangle_obtuse` spends to turn the resulting\nsum of volumes into the Λ-sum.\n\n**Nothing in `qou-w3eg`'s H4-5 arc remains open.**  This paragraph previously\nnamed `IdealTetEqChimney.idealTet_eq_chimney` as a residual gate, on the grounds\nthat it carries `α, β, γ < π/2` and is a set equality no continuation argument\ncould supply.  It carried them; it did not use them.  With the three unused\nhypotheses dropped, that theorem holds for every positive triple summing to `π`,\nand `ObtuseTiling.idealTetVolume_of_vertices_obtuse` states the identity on the\nvertex-defined region for `π/2 < γ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.third_angle_acute_of_obtuse","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.third_angle_acute_of_obtuse","kind":"theorem","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/IdealTetObtuseReduction.lean#L102-L107","doc":"**The reflected argument is acute.**  If `γ ≥ π/2` then `α + β = π − γ ≤ π/2`.\nThis is what makes the `−Λ(α+β)` term of `lob_sum_eq_reflected` an\nacute-argument Lobachevsky value in the obtuse case. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Archimedean.at_most_one_obtuse","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Archimedean.at_most_one_obtuse","kind":"theorem","line":94,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Archimedean/IdealTetObtuseReduction.lean#L94-L100","doc":"**An admissible angle triple has at most one obtuse angle.**  If two of\n`α, β, γ > 0` summing to `π` were both `≥ π/2`, the third would be `≤ 0`.  So\nMilnor's obtuse case is a single configuration, not a family. ","signatureChunk":0}]}